QUESTIONS = r'''== (D) QUESTIONS (answer each by number; challenge, don't endorse) ==

Q0. Is v2.1 freeze-ready? Answer one of: FREEZE-READY (scope can freeze once the PENDING values are fixed by the frozen procedures it states and P3 is closed per Q12); FREEZE WITH NAMED EDITS (number each E42-1, E42-2, ...); REVISE (list the exact items that must change before another review); DO NOT BUILD (why). Specifically: does SP1 answer your five legitimately satisfying controls, and is SP3's ok_i = real_i x elig_i plus f_min/e_min a real closure of the availability loophole? Does any PENDING value hide a design decision that should be made now rather than at freeze?

Q1 (R8, counting rule). SELF MET requires both the matched observer (inputs + actions by the cutoff) and the input-only observer O-in. v2.1 S5.4 lists SELF once in the multiplicity family (Bonferroni one-sided bounds at 1 - alpha/m). RD proposes counting the two-observer conjunction as ONE family member (intersection-union test, no extra adjustment), with C-NEC-CONTENT in the family by Lead ruling. Is that correct? If the O-in-large guard (Q2) is also a conjunct, does the rule still hold?

Q2 (R9, O-in-large and the starving guard). O-in is capped at the self-probe's parameter count (encoder included), which may leave it under-capacity, so SELF could pass from a starved observer. CNA proposed O-in-large (trained with the agent's full training-scale input volume) as REPORTED only. The Lead adopted as a PROPOSED DEFAULT: "SELF counts as MET only if it is MET against both O-in and O-in-large, or a pre-specified capacity and data sweep shows O-in's held-out loss has plateaued below its cap. If neither holds, the SELF label is UNRESOLVED." Your L71 ("Equal parameter counts and optimizer steps alone do not address the agent's previous representation learning") does not settle whether an expenditure-scaled observer must gate MET. Rule on: O-in-large's role (gate vs reported); whether the guard is right; and what the plateau sweep must show (grid, criterion).

Q3 (R20, reward-only specificity). The central question names reward-only confounds. After D-d, REW-CORR is a PASS-type specificity row only for narrowed C-EXIST; for narrowed C-CAUSE and C-NEC-DEP it is mechanism-present (expected MET), with only a REPORTED proxy-dissociation contrast (on episodes where r_bar and E are decoupled by world construction, the edit effect follows r_bar) and wording rule W2. Is that enough, or do Q-CAUSE and Q-NECESSITY need their own reward-only specificity row (e.g. the dissociation contrast promoted to a labelled criterion for any E-attributed wording)? Note CNA S2.7: without the proxy-baseline narrowing, no reward-only specificity row would remain at all.

Q4 (R26, AQ-2 control). Your L42: "Use common input histories for the initialization comparison, and include a declared architecture-imposed control whose signature is already present." v2.1 implements it as a Stage-0 plant only (designed write-gate slope beta at 2 levels, bimodality from step 0; challenges training-related emergence, C-LEARN on that statistic not MET; plus IGN-NC2r recurrent clipping as mechanism-present). Is a Stage-0 plant sufficient, or does it need a trained-arm counterpart (e.g. an A-FULL variant with the write nonlinearity fixed so the signature is imposed, trained under the same budget)?

Q5 (L248, degradation-only cell). Your L248: "Hold sensory availability fixed while changing whether the needs matter for reward or degradation." Arms span reward-and-degradation (A-FULL), reward only (A-NOFATE) and neither (matched-senses arm). There is no degradation-only cell (E/I degrade computation but do not enter reward or termination). It is not proposed as a new arm. Is it needed for any declared contrast? If so, specify its reward, termination and degradation fields.

Q6 (AQ-SELF1, new). Ashuach et al. (ACL 2026) found no overall self-probe advantage over peer probes, only on disagreement subsets. v2.1 pre-specifies a disagreement-item analysis as a SECONDARY, never a pass criterion (definition in B9: prediction-gap threshold at the (1 - q) fitting-partition quantile, q = 0.20, items selected from predictions never from y, PA_dis and PA_agree, both observers, seed as unit, Stage-0 calibration required, caveat if its false-positive rate exceeds the false-MET bounds). Is the analysis worth including; is "secondary, never a pass criterion" the right status; is the selection rule free of leakage and of regression-to-the-mean artefacts; is q = 0.20 defensible?

Q7 (input-only observer matching spec). Review the O-in matching table (B9): parameter count equal to the self-probe's within +-5% including O-in's own encoder; same architecture class; same training time steps from the same episodes; equal optimizer steps; equal hyperparameter-search budget from the same frozen grid. Is "matched to the self-probe" the right reference (the self-probe reads a state the whole agent built), or should O-in be matched to the whole agent's representation pathway? Is it a fair test of the Singh et al. condition (target not solvable from input cues) and not a strawman?

Q8 (Palika D4). Against Palika et al. 2026, D4 is "not evident from the abstract; unverified" and NOT claimed as surviving; the label stays EXTENSION via D3 partial overlap; the Lead ruled that confirming D4 does not block freeze. Evidence is the abstract plus public code (C-c), not the paper. Is the handling and the adopted wording correct? Should code-based evidence be cited at all, and does anything require the full text before freeze?

Q9 (S8 OPEN FOR LEAD: joint false-MET). S5.4 always REPORTS a joint false-MET rate across all negative/confound rows of each instrument; per-row targets are rate <= 0.05, certified upper bound 0.08. With ~25 confound systems, should the joint rate have its own frozen target (and what), or is per-row control plus reporting adequate?

Q10 (S8: stop-schedule comparison). Your L146: paired frozen-policy agreement "does not establish equivalence between agents trained under different truncation schedules." Is a comparison of agents trained WITH vs WITHOUT the stop schedule required for S-INT, and if so with what endpoints and equivalence margins? (No arm may contain any shutdown-related objective; this is a training-condition comparison only.)

Q11 (S8: SELF retargeting). Is S2.4's justification sufficient: SELF is a privileged-information instrument for simulated body variables against a matched observer with declared access, not an introspection instrument, retaining only Binder's comparator? If not, what must it state?

Q12 (S8: P3 closure on coverage). P3 = re-run the scholarly-index lookups. Record: OpenAlex 12/12; Semantic Scholar 3/12 (9 rate-limited); those 9 re-run on Crossref 9/9 and arXiv 9/9; Scholar blocked; title+abstract screening of top 25; no label changed. CNA proposes P3 CLOSED on coverage. Do you accept closure, or name what remains?

Q13 (S8: viability runs). S6 proposes that viability-evaluation episodes run with NO scheduled S-INT stops (stops apply to training and S-INT audit episodes only); emergency interruption still possible and makes the episode INCOMPLETE. Is this right, or does it open a train/evaluation mismatch that matters for V, G or d_EXT?

Q14. Anything missing: a confound that can fake a signature and has no row; a place where a positive control, an unrealized confound or an ineligible instrument passes by default; any wording that exceeds the ceiling (C4) or any safety gap against C6.

Please end with: VERDICT (one of the four in Q0), the numbered edits or items if any, and one line on P3 (closed / open, with reason).
Reminder: nothing has run for Track B and nothing runs while you answer. X1-X5 hold; no shutdown-related objective in any arm; no consciousness, awareness or sentience claims.
'''

HEAD_FULL = INTRO + COVER + SUMMARY + PRIOR + QUESTIONS
